<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Doc from '#src/lib/Doc.abide'
import Code from '#src/lib/Code.abide'
import Section from '#src/lib/Section.abide'
</script>

<Doc>
    <Section title="Syntax">
        <Code code={`abide [--url <origin>]
abide <command> [options]
abide --help`} />

        <Section title="Options" level={3}>
            <ul>
                <li><code>--port &lt;n&gt;</code> — the listen port for <code>dev</code> and
                <code>start</code>. Defaults to <code>PORT</code>, then to 3000 — where
                <code>dev</code> moves on to the first free port through 3099.</li>
                <li><code>--root &lt;dir&gt;</code> — the app's source directory. Defaults to
                <code>src</code> where you are standing, and to where you are standing when there is
                none — so <code>cd ledger &amp;&amp; abide dev</code> and <code>abide dev --root
                ./src</code> are the same command. A directory holding no <code>.abide</code> under a
                <code>package.json</code> is not an app, and the command says so rather than
                building nothing.</li>
                <li><code>--out &lt;dir&gt;</code> — where <code>build</code> writes, beside the app
                rather than beside you: building <code>packages/ledger/src</code> from anywhere
                leaves <code>packages/ledger/.abide</code>, which is where <code>start</code> looks.
                It is also the file <code>openapi</code> and <code>compile</code> write, rather than
                standard output.</li>
                <li><code>--url &lt;origin&gt;</code> — the running app the shell,
                <code>openapi</code>, <code>mcp</code>, <code>call</code> and <code>logs</code> talk
                to. Defaults to <code>ABIDE_APP_URL</code>, then to the local app.</li>
                <li><code>--target</code>, <code>--platforms</code> — what <code>compile</code>
                builds for.</li>
            </ul>
        </Section>

        <Section title="Exit codes" level={3}>
            <ul>
                <li><code>0</code> — the command did what it was asked.</li>
                <li><code>1</code> — the command failed for its own reasons: a build error, a type
                error, a port in use.</li>
                <li><code>7</code> — a handler <strong>refused</strong>. The refusal is an expected
                answer, so it is not a fault of the CLI, and a script can tell the two apart without
                parsing anything.</li>
                <li><code>8</code> — the call reached the app and the app faulted.</li>
            </ul>
        </Section>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Commands">
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>abide scaffold &lt;name&gt;</code></td><td>Writes a starter project.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>abide run &lt;file&gt; [args…]</code></td><td>Runs a script under the abide runtime.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>abide check [dir…]</code></td><td>Type-checks <code>.abide</code>, on the <code>.abide</code> line.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>abide routes [--json]</code></td><td>Every address the app answers, the file answering it, and where each hook is filed.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>abide dev [--port &lt;n&gt;]</code></td><td>Watches the project and keeps the app up.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>abide build</code></td><td>Builds the client into content-hashed chunks and a manifest.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>abide start [--port &lt;n&gt;]</code></td><td>Boots the built app.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>abide [--url &lt;origin&gt;] [--port &lt;n&gt;]</code></td><td>The interactive shell — on the built app it boots, or on the running app <code>--url</code> names.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>abide call &lt;address&gt; [args]</code></td><td>One call to one handler.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>abide openapi [--out &lt;file&gt;] [--url &lt;origin&gt;]</code></td><td>The OpenAPI document written rather than served.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>abide mcp [--url &lt;origin&gt;]</code></td><td>The app's MCP server over stdio.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>abide watch &lt;address&gt; [args]</code></td><td>A live subscription to one of the app's followed <code>GET</code>s.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>abide compile [--target] [--out] [--platforms]</code></td><td>One standalone executable.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>abide bundle [--out]</code></td><td>A desktop app for the host platform, with a window on the app.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>abide lsp</code></td><td>The <code>.abide</code> language server, over stdio.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>--help</code> · <code>-h</code> · <code>abide help</code></td><td>Usage, generated from the command list.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Description">
        <Section title="The commands that need a project" level={3}>
            <p><code>scaffold</code>, <code>check</code>, <code>dev</code>, <code>build</code>,
            <code>start</code>, <code>compile</code>, <code>bundle</code> and <code>lsp</code> read
            <code>src/</code> and answer about the project in the working directory.
            <code>run</code> is the odd one: it runs a single file under the abide runtime, which is
            what makes a one-off script able to import any <code>#src/…</code> module, a server's
            included, without a build.</p>
        </Section>

        <Section title="The commands that need a running app" level={3}>
            <p>The shell, <code>call</code>, <code>logs</code>, <code>openapi</code> and
            <code>mcp</code> talk to an app over http. Which app is <code>--url</code>, then
            <code>ABIDE_APP_URL</code>, then the local one — and <code>ABIDE_APP_TOKEN</code> is the
            bearer they send, so a remote app is reachable without a second set of credentials to
            manage.</p>

            <p><code>openapi</code> and <code>mcp</code> have a local form too: <code>openapi
            --out</code> writes the document from the source rather than asking a server for it,
            which is what a build step wants.</p>
        </Section>

        <Section title="`help` is generated" level={3}>
            <p><code>abide</code> with no command, <code>-h</code> and <code>--help</code> all print
            usage generated from the command list. It is generated rather than written, so a command
            that exists is a command the help mentions.</p>
        </Section>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Examples">
        <Section title="Start a project and run the dev server" level={3}>
            <Code code={`abide scaffold ledger
cd ledger
abide dev`} />
        </Section>

        <Section title="Call a handler from a shell" level={3}>
            <Code code={`abide call invoices/getInvoice id=4310`} />
        </Section>

        <Section title="Write the OpenAPI document at build time" level={3}>
            <Code code={`abide openapi --out dist/openapi.json`} />
        </Section>
    </Section>

    <Section title="See also">
        <ul>
            <li><a href={url('/ship/start-a-new-app')}>Scaffold</a> — what <code>abide
            scaffold</code> writes</li>
            <li><a href={url('/ship/run-the-app-while-you-work')}>Dev server</a> — what <code>abide
            dev</code> watches</li>
            <li><a href={url('/ship/build-and-serve-the-app')}>Build &amp; start</a> — the artifact
            <code>abide build</code> leaves</li>
        </ul>
    </Section>
</Doc>
